  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 019: Photoresist converts a lithography image into a temporary chemical stencil, and its sensitivity can influence both yield and scanner throughput.

Lesson 020: Overlay measures whether a new pattern lands in the right place relative to earlier layers, making alignment a system-wide yield constraint.

Lesson 021: Critical-dimension control asks whether printed features have the correct width and geometry, because tiny size errors can alter electrical behavior.

Lesson 022: Lithography is a closed control loop linking resolution, resist, overlay, CD, etch and metrology rather than a single scanner exposure.

Lesson 023: Multiple patterning creates denser structures by splitting one logical layer into several physical patterning steps, trading resolution for more process complexity.

The big idea

EUV lithography has a strange tradeoff:

**more photons usually make the printed pattern more reliable — but delivering those photons takes time.**

So the same EUV scanner can produce different wafer output depending on the exposure dose required by the resist and pattern.

That means counting scanners is not enough to understand EUV capacity.

A better mental model is:

**effective EUV capacity ≈ installed scanners × wafers per hour × availability × utilization**

And even that must be adjusted for how many EUV layers each wafer needs.

## 1. What “dose” means

During exposure, the scanner delivers EUV energy to the photoresist.

The **dose** is the amount of exposure energy delivered per unit area, commonly measured in millijoules per square centimeter.

Higher dose means the resist receives more photon-driven chemical events before the scanner moves on.

Why might that help?

Because photons arrive statistically. At very small dimensions, a feature may depend on a relatively small number of useful photon events. Random variation can therefore become large enough that the feature prints incorrectly.

Those random local failures are part of what engineers call **stochastics**.

Think of flipping a coin. If you flip it 10,000 times, the result is very predictable. If you flip it four times, randomness dominates.

Tiny EUV features have a similar problem: too little photon information can produce missing contacts, broken lines, bridges, rough edges or local dimension errors even when the scanner itself is well calibrated.

## 2. The dose-throughput tradeoff

Suppose one pattern prints reliably at 20 mJ/cm², while another needs 30 mJ/cm² to suppress defects.

The second pattern requires 50% more exposure energy.

All else equal, that means more exposure time and therefore fewer wafers per hour.

ASML’s older NXE:3400C specification made this relationship unusually visible: at 20 mJ/cm² it specified at least 170 wafers per hour, while at 30 mJ/cm² it specified at least 135 wafers per hour.

Do not treat those exact numbers as a rule for every modern tool. Newer systems have more source power, faster stages and better productivity.

The important relationship is:

**higher required dose can consume scanner capacity.**

